# Defect Registry [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

> A fix-resistant defect is a PROCESS signal, and a process signal needs a durable, QUERYABLE home. The registry keys a defect to a STABLE SYMPTOM IDENTITY (what broke, not when it was filed), so a recurrence dedups onto the SAME node and ACCUMULATES: every prior fix attempt, its root-cause hypothesis, WHY IT DIDN'T HOLD, and its kin. A fresh defect-investigator runs `defect_query` FIRST and inherits that history instead of re-digging blind. The shape is deliberately two-halved along a clean seam — the RELATIONSHIPS + per-attempt narrative live in a FactLattice graph (defect-ops, the sister of decision-ops); the COUNTS + aggregation live in the retrospective SQLite (the existing `defect_investigations` table, [SPEC:CXC-DEFECT-STORE]). The MCP writes both on one call; a roll-up publisher projects the aggregate to the bus; a Dashboard widget renders it.

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DEFECT-STORE]
- status: draft — built 2026-07-26 on build/defect-registry, pending Architect glass
- premise: the recurrence GRAPH (which fix superseded which, why it didn't hold, which kin re-break together) is what a flat table cannot hold; the HISTOGRAM (which root-cause class dominates) is what a graph charts poorly. Keep each in its native shape, cross-linked by a stable id.

---

## 1. The graph/SQLite seam

### [DR-SEAM] Two halves, one seam, cross-linked by defect_id
- definition: the registry splits along data SHAPE. The GRAPH half (`defects/defect-graph.lts`, written by `composeio.defect_ops`) holds one `[DEFECT-<slug>]` node per defect plus `[DEFECT-<slug>-A<n>]` fix-attempt children carrying the per-attempt narrative (root-cause hypothesis, what changed, outcome, why it didn't hold) and the recurrence edges. The SQLite half (`defect_investigations` in `intelligence/retrospective/data/metrics.db`) holds one aggregate row per fix attempt whose by-root-cause histogram is the process-improvement signal.
- cross-link: each SQLite row carries a `defect_id` column (added additively, [DR-SQLITE]) = the graph DEFECT node id, so an aggregate row walks to its recurrence-graph node and back.
- rationale: "has this recurred, what earlier fix did it invalidate, why" is a typed graph (walkable edges between named nodes); "what class of defect dominates, is fork-divergence trending down" is a `GROUP BY` over uniform rows. Forcing both into one store cripples one — a table can't walk a recurrence chain; a hand-curated graph can't be charted or windowed cheaply. This is the same split [SPEC:CXC-DEFECT-STORE] ratified (SQLite-primary + LTS-complement), realized as a LIVE, tool-driven corpus.
- boundary: EVERY fix attempt writes the SQLite row (cheap, mandatory — the histogram wants them all). The graph is the curated narrative — one node per real defect, accumulating.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

### [DR-IDENTITY] Stable symptom identity — the load-bearing idea
- requirement: a defect MUST be keyed to WHAT BROKE, not to when it was filed. `defect_ops.profile.symptom_slug()` derives a deterministic `DEFECT-<TOKENS>-<HASH6>` id over the NORMALIZED symptom text (lowercased, punctuation-stripped, whitespace-collapsed): the same symptom filed twice, in any casing/spacing/punctuation, lands on ONE node.
- rationale: dedup-by-identity is what makes accumulation possible. Without it, a recurrence files a fresh, historyless record and the pattern is lost — exactly the blindness this registry closes. The hash tail makes two genuinely different symptoms that share leading words still distinct.
- test (machine): `symptom_slug("X")` is stable across casing/spacing/punctuation; a repeat `defect_report` for the same symptom dedups to one node and bumps report-count (tests/test_defect_ops.py).
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

### [DR-GRAPH-SHAPE] The graph node + attempt-child shape
- definition: a `[DEFECT-<slug>]` node carries `lifecycle-position` (reported | investigating | fix-attempted | verified | recurred), `first-reported`, `last-activity`, `report-count`, `attempt-count`, `symptom` (the original human text), optional `area`, and optional kin edges. Each `[DEFECT-<slug>-A<n>]` attempt child carries `outcome` (held | recurred), the root-cause hypothesis, what changed, why it didn't hold, `branch`/`commit`, and its edges.
- key-insight: attempts are FIRST-CLASS graph nodes, not table rows, precisely so the per-attempt narrative + the supersedes/reopens edges live in the graph and are walkable from the defect. `defect_query` assembles the defect + its attempt children (in order) + kin — that assembly IS the anti-re-dig payload.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

### [DR-EDGES] The recurrence edges — domain names mapped onto core verbs
- requirement: the recurrence relationships MUST be REAL, dangle-CHECKED FactLattice edges, not prose-with-brackets, so the no-forward-dangle guard actually enforces them. The FactLattice core generates edges only from a fixed property-verb set ([DEC-ONE-FACTLATTICE-PARSER] parser `REL_PROPERTY_EDGES`) and is NOT extended by a profile, so the defect domain's edge kinds are AUTHORED with core verbs and the domain names map onto them:
  - `supersedes` -> **SUPERSEDES** (core): a NEW fix-attempt supersedes the prior attempt — the recurrence chain, walkable to see how many times an area re-broke.
  - `investigates` (the parent link) -> **part-of** / PART_OF (core): an attempt is part-of its defect; keeps attempts walkable from the defect and dangle-checked.
  - `related-to` (kin) -> **see-also** / RELATED_TO (core): kin defects in the same area; a kin target that resolves nowhere is REFUSED by the guard.
  - `reopens`: an intra-defect property on a recurred attempt (targets the existing parent, so it never dangles); the recurrence signal is ALSO carried by the defect lifecycle -> `recurred`.
- rationale: reuse the engine's enforced verbs rather than mint synonyms it won't check. The defect profile (`defect-profile.yaml`) documents the domain vocabulary; the graph uses the core spellings so the guarantees are real.
- test (machine): a `related_to`/kin edge to a missing node is refused + rolled back (the defect is NOT created); an attempt's `supersedes` points at the prior attempt (tests/test_defect_ops.py).
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

---

## 2. defect-ops — the deterministic CORE + MCP (the sister of decision-ops)

### [DR-CORE] The single-writer CORE, reusing decision_ops by reference
- requirement: `composeio.defect_ops` (report / attempt / resolve / query) MUST be the single writer over the defect graph, with the SAME guarantees as `decision_ops`: append-only (resolve ANNOTATES, never deletes), lint-before/lint-after with a content-keyed rollback on any NEW forward dangle / collision / parse error, and idempotent no-ops that push no change.
- key-mechanism: it does NOT re-implement `.lts` parsing, node splicing, or the guard — it CONSUMES the decision_ops core primitives verbatim (`render_node`, `find_node_span`, `_apply_with_guard`, the splice helpers) and reuses `decision_ops._enginepath` (the lts-engine path seat) and `decision_ops.lts_refresh.LtsRefresher` (refresh-on-write) by reference. It is a NEW CONSUMER of the one FactLattice engine, never a fork.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

### [DR-MCP] The four MCP tools — an Intelligence drives them identically to a human
- definition: `composeio.defect_ops.mcp_server` is a thin stdio JSON-RPC MCP (byte-for-byte the decision_ops.mcp_server shape) over the core, with a transport-agnostic `dispatch(name, arguments) -> dict` seam so a future ComposeBus request/announcement handler swaps in without touching op logic (the credo).
  - `defect_query` — THE ANTI-RE-DIG GATE (read-only, no refresh): given a symptom or a DEFECT id, return the accumulated history — prior attempts + each attempt's root-cause hypothesis + WHY IT DIDN'T HOLD + related kin. A defect-investigator MUST run this FIRST.
  - `defect_report` — register a NEW defect OR append a report to an existing one (dedup by stable identity; bump report-count + dates; a report on a `verified` defect is a recurrence -> `recurred`).
  - `defect_attempt` — append a fix attempt (creating the defect if this is the first filing, so the parent edge never dangles); the new attempt SUPERSEDES the prior; a `recurred` outcome reopens the defect. ALSO writes the SQLite analytics row ([DR-SQLITE]).
  - `defect_resolve` — close (`verified`) or reopen (`reopened`) by annotation; ALSO writes a SQLite row.
- write path: call the core op; IFF `changed`, push an lts-engine refresh (reused LtsRefresher, area="defects") so the new node is immediately queryable with no stale window; on a refused/no-op, push nothing. A `DangleRefusedError` returns `{success: false, refused: true, problems}`.
- test (machine): the selftest (`python3 -m composeio.defect_ops --selftest`) drives report->query->attempt->attempt->resolve through the dispatch seam against a TEMP corpus + TEMP db — 20/20, incl. accumulation, the supersedes chain, refusal, the SQLite cross-link, and a fresh refresh verdict.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

### [DR-CLI] One composition, every entry point — the CLI is a thin argv->dispatch shell
- requirement: the write-both-seams-and-refresh operation (core op -> engine refresh -> SQLite analytics row) MUST be composed in exactly ONE place — `DefectOpsMcp.dispatch(...)` — and reused BY REFERENCE by every entry point. The CLI (`python3 -m composeio.defect_ops <cmd>`, the surface the defect-investigator agent-def actually invokes for report/query/attempt/resolve) MUST route through that same dispatch — it is a thin argv->dispatch shell exactly as the stdio server's `serve()` is a thin stdin->dispatch shell. NO entry point may call the core ops directly: the core writes ONLY the graph, so a direct-to-core entry point composes a PARTIAL operation that silently drops the SQLite analytics row ([DR-SQLITE]) and the engine refresh ([DR-MCP] write path). This is reuse-by-reference-never-fork ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]).
- rationale: this closes the fork-divergence CLASS, not one instance. The SQLite bridge + the engine refresh were built into the MCP dispatch, but the CLI forked a direct-to-core path that dropped both — so every defect-investigator that filed an attempt via the documented CLI wrote the graph and left `defect_investigations` EMPTY (0 rows). The `defect_metrics_publisher` then projected an all-NULL roll-up and the Dashboard widget showed blanks, even though defects HAD been investigated (the recurrence graph held them). A second entry point that re-composes the operation is precisely how a seam gets dropped and a fix "recurs".
- class-defaulting: the dispatch defaults the analytics `root_cause_class` to the attempt's `root_cause_hypothesis` (the agent-def passes the taxonomy as `--root-cause`) when no explicit class is given, and DERIVES the `under_spec`/`fork` structural flags from that class — so even a minimal CLI attempt populates the histogram + the under-spec/fork gauge from the SAME call.
- test (machine): a CROSS-ENTRY-POINT test (`tests/test_defect_ops_cli_bridge.py`) is PARAMETRIZED over the entry points (CLI argv shell + MCP dispatch) and asserts EACH lands a `defect_investigations` row cross-linked by `defect_id` and feeds the roll-up — so any future entry point that drops the SQLite seam fails loudly. It fails before the de-fork (CLI wrote 0 rows) and passes after.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

---

## 3. The SQLite wiring — reuse the existing recorder, add one column

### [DR-SQLITE] defect_id cross-link on the existing defect_investigations recorder
- requirement: `defect_attempt` (and `defect_resolve`) MUST write an aggregate row via the EXISTING `intelligence.retrospective.db.record_defect_investigation(...)` — reuse, never rebuild ([SPEC:CXC-DEFECT-STORE] owns the table + `defects_by_root_cause`). The ONLY schema change is an additive `defect_id TEXT` column carrying the graph DEFECT node id (the cross-link), landed by an idempotent `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`-safe `ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN` migration mirroring `_migrate_away_watch`.
- boundary/error: the SQLite write is BEST-EFFORT and NEVER fails the graph write (which already committed) — a missing module or unwritable DB logs and returns None. The graph is the source of truth for the narrative; SQLite is the aggregate.
- default: `defect_id` defaults to `''` (old rows read NULL-safe); `recurrence` on an attempt row = (outcome == recurred); `resolution` = what-changed.
- test (machine): a row records + reads back its `defect_id`; the additive migration preserves legacy rows; `defects_by_root_cause()` counts the attempts (intelligence/retrospective/tests/test_defect_investigations.py).
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

---

## 4. The roll-up publisher + the Dashboard widget

### [DR-PUBLISHER] defect_metrics_publisher -> self/<id>/defects
- requirement: `intelligence/tools/defect_metrics_publisher.py` (mirroring `away_watch_publisher`, project-only — no launch leg) MUST read `defects_by_root_cause()` + `list_defect_investigations()` + the defect GRAPH (fix-resistant leaders = defects with attempt-count >= 2) and publish a roll-up to `self/<id>/defects` (default id `architect` -> `self/architect/defects`, the same `self/*` scope the other publishers use). Registered as an in-process, session-independent source in `intelligence/console/data_sources.py`.
- payload: `{ts, total, by_root_cause:{under-spec, fork-divergence, ...}, structural:{under_spec, fork, recurrence}, recurrence_rate, fix_resistant:[{symptom, attempts, status}]}`.
- default: the GRAPH read is best-effort — if the engine isn't importable in-process, the fix-resistant leaders fall back to a SQLite group-by-`defect_id` (count >= 2). The SQLite aggregate is the load-bearing signal; the graph adds the human-readable symptom + live status.
- test (machine): the publisher selftest (7/7) proves the empty-store zero roll-up, the taxonomy histogram, the structural tallies, the recurrence rate, and the fix-resistant leaders (SQLite fallback).
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

### [DR-WIDGET] The Defects dashboard widget
- requirement: `DefectsSummaryWidget` (`python/composeio/render/widgets/defects_summary.py`) MUST subscribe `self/architect/defects` and render the roll-up: total investigated, the 3-cell root-cause gauge (under-spec / fork / recurrence), the recurrence rate, and the fix-resistant leaders (symptom + attempt-count + status). Registered in `widget_registry` + `build_leaf` + `widgets/__init__` with `category dashboard-eligible`, `region_affinity {dashboard}`, and a dashboard-sized `min_size` (360x220) so the fit-aware placement steers it onto the composable Dashboard grid ([DEC-WIDGET-PLACEMENT-CALLS-SETTLED] D1).
- boundary: data-bound (subscribes; never reads SQLite/graph directly — the perceive/present split) and change-gated (`request_redraw()` only, only on a real content change — [CXC-R-DAMAGE-ONCHANGE]).
- test (machine): widget tests (10/10) cover payload coercion, the 3-cell tallies, the recurrence-rate string, empty-state dashes, damage discipline, and the registry descriptor (dashboard-eligible + affinity {dashboard} + min-width > 332). Visual proof: `python/composeio/render/examples/defects_summary_proof.png`.
- verify-glass: the human/architect PLACES the widget on the live Dashboard grid (the composable placement is a config artifact + a glass action) and confirms it renders the roll-up on the running console — machine-green (renders with fixture data) is not glass-green (placed + live on the broker-connected console).
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

---

## 5. Decision record

### [DR-CHANGE] Why the registry has this shape
- change: Built the Defect Registry as a graph/SQLite seam — a live defect-ops MCP (report/query/attempt/resolve) over a `defects/defect-graph.lts` corpus (the recurrence graph), cross-linked to the EXISTING `defect_investigations` SQLite recorder by an additive `defect_id` column, projected to the bus by a roll-up publisher and rendered by a Dashboard widget. defect-ops mirrors decision-ops and reuses its splice/guard/refresh by reference (one FactLattice engine, a new consumer).
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "[SPEC:CXC-DEFECT-STORE] defined the two-part shape (SQLite-primary aggregate + LTS-complement [DEFECT-*] node) but the LTS half was a hand-authored CONVENTION and the SQLite table was built-but-UNWIRED (0 rows). A recurring defect therefore still lost its history between defect-investigator dispatches — each fresh investigator re-dug blind and missed the pattern."
    reasoning: "The anti-re-dig gate needs a WRITE surface + a QUERY surface, not just a table shape. Mirroring decision-ops gives that for free — the same single-writer discipline (append-only, no-forward-dangle, refresh-on-write) applied to a defect corpus. Keeping the aggregate in the existing recorder (adding one column) avoids a second store and keeps the histogram [SPEC:CXC-DEFECT-STORE] already owns. The graph makes the recurrence chain walkable; the histogram makes the process lever chartable."
    rejected_alternative: "SQLite-only (no graph) — re-creates the exact blindness: a table can't walk 'attempt A2 superseded A1 which superseded the commit fix', so the '5 attempts all touched X' pattern stays invisible. A SECOND FactLattice engine for defects — violates [DEC-ONE-FACTLATTICE-PARSER]; defect-ops is a consumer. A profile-declared edge verb set — the core only edges a fixed verb set, so custom verbs wouldn't be dangle-checked; authored on core verbs instead ([DR-EDGES])."
    counter_argument: "Two stores risk drift — a graph defect with no SQLite row, or a row with no node."
    counter_response: "Bounded by the MCP: `defect_attempt` writes BOTH on one call (the SQLite write is best-effort so it never blocks the committed graph write), and the `defect_id` column cross-links them. Drift is a missing best-effort row, never a lost graph write."
    invalidating_assumption: "That defect volume stays low enough for the graph to be tool-curated one node per real defect. If defects become high-volume, revisit: auto-skeleton nodes from SQLite rows, or promote to the full DELTA machinery ([SPEC:CXC-DEFECT-STORE] §8.3 note)."
    implication: "defect_ops joins decision_ops as a sibling single-writer; the defect-investigator agent's FIRST action becomes defect_query and its deliverable becomes defect_attempt/defect_resolve (agent-def change, David's glass); the registry is seeded EMPTY (the architect files the first real defect)."
- relates-to: [SPEC:CXC-DEFECT-STORE], [DEC-DEFECT-TRACKING-STORE], [DEC-DEFECT-INVESTIGATOR-AGENT], [DEC-ONE-FACTLATTICE-PARSER]
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

### [DR-OPEN] Open / glass — FLAGGED for the Architect, NOT resolved here
- The defect-investigator AGENT-DEF change (make defect_query its first action + defect_attempt/defect_resolve its write-back) is David's glass — this build supplies the tools, not the agent rewiring.
- LIVE placement of the Defects widget on the console Dashboard grid + confirming it renders on the broker-connected console is a human glass action ([DR-WIDGET] verify-glass) — machine-green != glass-green.
- Whether defect_ops becomes the SOLE writer of the defect corpus (vs a hand-append path) mirrors decision_ops' unresolved live-cutover — David's call, not a build default.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-DEFECT-REGISTRY]

